WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.929 --> 00:00:06.229
Armed guards and heightened security checks
didn't deter the thousands of people who travelled

00:00:06.229 --> 00:00:07.830
to be here.

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:12.040
Many came to see a man who - despite having
left public office more than four months ago

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:15.120
- was still treated to a rock star's welcome.

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:21.880
In that time, Barack Obama's high regard for
the German Chancellor hasn't changed.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:24.580
"First of all ‘Guten Tag’.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.140
It's good to see all of you.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:28.980
It's good to be back in Berlin.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.280
I have to say first of all.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:40.360
Not only do I love the city but one of my
favourite partners throughout my presidency

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.809
is sitting next to me today.

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:45.600
Chancellor Merkel has done outstandig work.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.160
Not just here in Germany but around the world."

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.700
Small talk soon gives way to serious reflection.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:58.660
Tight security wasn't the only shadow the
Manchester bombing cast over this gathering.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.420
"The deterrence strategy that worked in the
Cold War had its dark side, but it prevented

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:08.990
numerous conflicts from escalating.

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:12.640
Not least because both sides were attached
to life itself.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.800
That's not true of terrorist groups.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:22.920
They're ready to sacrifice their own lives
to destroy those of others -- as we saw in Manchester."

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:28.520
Terrorism and war also preoccupied the people
invited to pose questions.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.800
"Why did a Christian and Nobel Prize laureate,
repeatedly authorize drone attacks,

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:36.920
despite civilian casualties?"

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.540
Obama gave a frank response.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:49.200
"In some cases - I know for a fact - that
actions that I took might have resultated

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:51.189
in civilian casualities.

00:01:51.189 --> 00:01:53.220
Often times as a consequence of error."

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:58.160
Defending new restrictions on immigration,
Merkel said

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.360
Germany must resort to deportation when needed.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:06.620
"That's a dilemma we have to bear.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.280
We have to make the right choices about who
must go.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:13.420
And on the question of fairness, I can only
say, we have to make those choices quickly."

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:19.500
Such words from the former President and the
most powerful woman in Europe

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.240
resonated with listeners.

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:28.180
“It's important to convey the sense that
politics doesn't just take place far away

00:02:28.189 --> 00:02:29.189
from the public.

00:02:29.189 --> 00:02:33.680
It's actually a dialogue between citizens
and in response to their concerns.”

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.940
“I thought it was good that he said war
should always be only the last resort, and

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:42.400
that each of us can do our bit to make the
world a better place."

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:51.180
And that indeed was the message here: that everyone
can be a part of hope and change.

